From e534083f051fc1b380fe2729b19c514ff3c72156 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matthew Dowell <mbd06b+github@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 09:53:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] feat: `brit rev-list --count`, Git's spelling of
 counting a revision walk

Requested by the integrating session (about 25 uses in the agents'
fallback census). A real clap subcommand whose help leads to
`brit revision list`; the walk is gix's `rev_walk` with hidden tips, the
same traversal `revision list` uses for `A..B`.

- `--count <rev>...` with `A`, `^A`, `A..B` and `A...B` (both sides minus
  the commits reachable from their merge bases) prints one number.
- `--left-right --count A...B` prints `<left>\t<right>`; without a
  symmetric range every commit counts on the right, as in Git.
- An unknown revision fails with Git's ambiguous-argument diagnostic and
  128; no revision is a usage error (129); listing without `--count` is
  refused with a pointer to `brit revision list` / `brit log`.
- `rev-list` joins the read-only arm of `requires_mutation_guard` (one
  appended pattern) and brit-cli's `READ_ONLY_COMMANDS`.

